1. Why is the redirect_uri parameter one of the most dangerous parts of OAuth?
	If OAuth authentication happens between the user, the authorization server, and the application, why is the redirect_uri parameter such a common attack vector?
Ans:- To answer this well, you must understand the OAuth authorization code flow.
Step-by-Step OAuth Flow:

Imagine a user logging into an app using OAuth 2.0 with OpenID Connect.
The login starts like this:
User Browser
     │
     ▼
Application
     │
     ▼
Authorization Server
The application redirects the user to the authorization server.
Example request:
https://auth.example.com/authorize?
client_id=123
&redirect_uri=https://app.example.com/callback
&response_type=code
&scope=openid

Here the redirect_uri tells the authorization server where to send the authorization code after login.
After the user authenticates:
Authorization Server
        │
        ▼
redirect_uri?code=AUTH_CODE
The code is sent back to the application.

-> Where the Problem Appears:
If the authorization server does not strictly validate redirect_uri, an attacker can manipulate it.
Example attack request:
https://auth.example.com/authorize?
client_id=123
&redirect_uri=https://evil.com/callback
Now the authorization server will redirect the user to the attacker:
https://evil.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The attacker receives the authorization code and exchanges it for tokens.
Now the attacker logs in as the victim.

-> Why This Happens:-
Many developers validate redirect URIs poorly.
Common mistakes:
• allowing wildcard domains
• using substring matching
• accepting any URL with the same domain

Example vulnerable check:
if redirect_uri contains "app.example.com"
Attacker bypass:
https://app.example.com.evil.com/callback

-> Correct Mitigation:
The authorization server must enforce:
• Exact redirect_uri match
• The redirect URI must be pre-registered and compared exactly.
• Example secure approach:
Registered:
https://app.example.com/callback
Allowed:
✔ exact match
Rejected:
✘ https://app.example.com.evil.com
✘ https://evil.com

-> The Key Insight:
Redirect URI validation protects authorization codes.
If this validation fails, the entire OAuth flow collapses.

2. Why is the state parameter critical in OAuth?
Ans:- The OAuth Login Flow:
When an application sends the user to the authorization server:
GET /authorize?
client_id=app123
&redirect_uri=https://app.example.com/callback
&state=xyz123
The state value is generated by the application.
It is stored in the user's session.
After authentication, the authorization server redirects:
https://app.example.com/callback?
code=AUTH_CODE
&state=xyz123
The application checks if the returned state matches the stored value.

-> What Happens If state Is Missing?
Without the state parameter, OAuth becomes vulnerable to login CSRF.
Attack scenario:
Attacker logs into the app with their own account.
They obtain:
https://app.example.com/callback?code=ATTACKER_CODE
They trick the victim into visiting that URL.
Now the victim's browser completes the login using the attacker's authorization code.
The victim unknowingly logs into the attacker's account.

-> Why This Is Dangerous:-
This sounds harmless, but it enables serious attacks.
Example:
• Attacker account contains malicious data.
• Victim uploads confidential files thinking it's their account.
• But everything goes to the attacker.

-> Correct Mitigation:
The application must verify:
returned_state == stored_state
The state should be:
• random
• unpredictable
• stored in session

-> Key Insight:
The state parameter prevents cross-site request forgery in OAuth flows.

3. Why is the Implicit Flow considered insecure today?
Older OAuth systems used implicit flow.
Example:
response_type=token

Instead of returning an authorization code, the authorization server returns the token directly.
https://app.example.com/callback#access_token=TOKEN

The token appears in the URL fragment.

-> Why This Was Designed:
Implicit flow was created for JavaScript applications that couldn't safely store client secrets.

-> Why It Became Dangerous:-
Tokens exposed in URLs create several leak points.
Example:
https://app.example.com/#access_token=XYZ
This token can leak through:
Browser history
Referrer headers
Logs
Malicious JavaScript
Third-party scripts

-> Real Attack Scenario:
•  User logs into an application.
•  The page loads third-party analytics:
<script src="analytics.js">
•  If analytics logs URLs, the token gets captured.

-> Modern Solution:
OAuth now recommends:
Authorization Code Flow + PKCE

PKCE prevents code interception.
Flow:
Browser
   │
   ▼
Authorization Server
   │
   ▼
Return Authorization Code
   │
   ▼
App exchanges code with PKCE verifier

Even if code is stolen, attacker cannot exchange it.

-> Key Insight:-
Implicit flow was replaced because tokens should never travel in browser URLs.

4. Why is ID Token validation often implemented incorrectly?
OIDC adds identity on top of OAuth 2.0.
The identity information comes in an ID Token, which is a JSON Web Token.
Example ID token:
header.payload.signature

Payload:
{
 "iss": "https://auth.example.com",
 "aud": "client123",
 "exp": 1712345678,
 "sub": "user123"
}

-> Common Developer Mistake:-
Developers only decode the JWT.
Example:
const user = decode(id_token)
They trust the payload.
But they never verify the signature.

-> Attack Scenario:-
Attacker creates fake token:
{
 "sub": "admin",
 "aud": "client123"
}
Signs it with their own key.
If the app does not verify the signature, the attacker becomes admin.

-> Correct Token Validation:-
Applications must validate:
signature
issuer
audience
expiration
nonce

Example checks:
iss == trusted issuer
aud == application client id
exp > current time

Key Insight:- 
JWT decoding ≠ JWT validation.
The signature verification is what makes the token trustworthy.

5. Why is OAuth token leakage via Referer headers a real risk?
a. First understand what a Referer header actually is:-
Whenever a browser loads a new resource (image, script, CSS, iframe, etc.), it sends the URL of the page that initiated the request.
Example:
You are on this page:
https://app.example.com/profile
The page loads an image:
<img src="https://cdn.images.com/logo.png">

When the browser requests the image, it automatically sends:
GET /logo.png
Host: cdn.images.com
Referer: https://app.example.com/profile

So the server hosting the image learns which page the user was visiting.
This is normal browser behavior used for analytics, security checks, and logging.

b. Now look at the OAuth callback URL:-
After login using OAuth 2.0, the authorization server redirects the user back to the application.
Example callback URL:
https://app.example.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE

Important part:
?code=AUTH_CODE

This authorization code is sensitive because it can be exchanged for tokens.

c. What happens when the callback page loads:-
When the callback page loads, the browser renders the page.
The page may load many external resources:
<script src="https://analytics.com/script.js"></script>
<img src="https://cdn.images.com/logo.png">
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css">

Now the browser requests these resources.

d. The browser automatically sends the Referer:-
When requesting the analytics script:
GET /script.js
Host: analytics.com
Referer: https://app.example.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
Now the third-party server receives the entire callback URL, including:
code=AUTH_CODE

This means the authorization code has leaked to another domain.

-> Why this becomes a security issue:
If the external domain is malicious (or compromised), it can capture the code.
Example log on attacker server:
Referer:
https://app.example.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
Now the attacker takes that code and sends a request to the OAuth token endpoint:
POST /token
grant_type=authorization_code
code=AUTH_CODE
client_id=app

If the app does not use PKCE, the attacker can obtain the access token.

-> Why This Is Dangerous?
If the attacker controls the third-party domain, they can capture codes.
They then exchange the code for tokens.

-> Mitigation:
i. Use PKCE : Even if the attacker steals the code, they cannot exchange it without the code verifier.
PKCE flow:
code + code_verifier required
The attacker only has the code.
ii. Remove the code immediately.
Applications often do:
/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
Then immediately redirect to:
/dashboard
So the code disappears from the URL.
iii. Referrer Policy:
Websites can instruct the browser not to send the full URL.
Example header:
Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
or
Referrer-Policy: origin
Now the browser sends:
Referer: https://app.example.com
instead of the full URL.

6. Why is mixing ID Tokens and Access Tokens a serious OIDC security mistake?
Why is it dangerous if an application uses an ID token to authorize API requests?
Many engineers confuse authentication and authorization here.
Step 1 — Understand the purpose of each token:
In OpenID Connect, two main tokens exist.
-> ID Token:
Purpose: authentication
It tells the client application:
This user has successfully logged in
Example payload:
{
 "sub": "user123",
 "email": "user@example.com",
 "iss": "https://auth.company.com",
 "aud": "web-client"
}
It proves identity.

-> Access Token:
Purpose: authorization
It tells APIs:
This client is allowed to access resources
Example:
{
 "scope": "payments.read",
 "aud": "payments-api"
}

Step 2 — The common developer mistake:
Developers sometimes send the ID token to APIs instead of the access token.
Example API request:
GET /api/payments
Authorization: Bearer ID_TOKEN
This happens because ID tokens are JWTs containing user info, which looks convenient.

Step 3 — Why this becomes dangerous:
ID tokens are issued for the client application, not the API.
So the audience field might be:
aud = web-client
But the API never checks this properly.
An attacker can obtain an ID token from another client.
Example:
client A -> gets ID token
client B API accepts it
This breaks token audience isolation.

Step 4 — Real attack scenario:
User
 │
 ▼
Login via Client A
 │
 ▼
Receives ID token (aud=clientA)
 │
 ▼
Attacker sends token to Client B API

If the API does not check the aud claim, it accepts the request.
Now the attacker can access protected APIs.

-> Correct mitigation:
APIs must:
• accept only access tokens
• validate aud claim
• verify issuer
• validate scope
Never use ID tokens for API authorization.

7. Why is the nonce parameter critical in OpenID Connect?
Step 1 — Understand what nonce does:
OIDC adds identity verification to OAuth.
During login the client sends:
/authorize?
client_id=123
nonce=abc123

The authorization server embeds the nonce in the ID token.
Example:
{
 "sub": "user123",
 "nonce": "abc123"
}

Step 2 — Why this matters:
The client must check:
returned_nonce == original_nonce
This prevents token replay attacks.

Step 3 — Attack scenario:
Imagine the attacker previously obtained a valid ID token:
ID_TOKEN for user123
Without nonce validation, the attacker can reuse it.
Attacker -> sends old ID token
Application -> accepts it
Now the attacker logs in as the victim.

Step 4 — Why developers forget this:
Developers think:
JWT signature validation is enough
But JWT validation does not prevent replay.
Nonce binds the token to a specific authentication request.

->Correct mitigation:-
Clients must verify:
nonce
issuer
audience
expiration
signature

8. Why is the audience claim critical for API security?
This question tests token confusion attacks.
Step 1 — OAuth tokens can target different services
Large companies often have multiple APIs.
Example architecture:
Auth Server
   │
   ├── Payments API
   ├── Profile API
   └── Admin API

Each API expects tokens meant for it.
Example token:
aud = payments-api

Step 2 — Misconfiguration
Some APIs validate only:
signature
issuer
But ignore audience.

Step 3 — Attack
Attacker obtains token for a less privileged API.
aud = profile-api
Then sends it to another API.
GET /admin/deleteUser
Authorization: Bearer profile-token

If the admin API ignores the aud claim, it accepts the request.
Result:
Privilege escalation across services.
This is called token substitution attack.

-> Correct mitigation:
APIs must validate:
aud == expected API
Each API must reject tokens meant for other services.

9. What is the OAuth “confused deputy problem”?
The idea:
A confused deputy is a trusted service tricked into misusing its privileges.
In OAuth this happens when:
Client app uses attacker-controlled tokens

-> Example architecture:
User
 │
 ▼
Client Application
 │
 ▼
API

The client receives tokens and calls APIs.

-> Attack scenario:
Attacker authenticates with their account and obtains:
access_token_attacker
Then they send this token to the victim’s browser through a crafted link.
The victim’s browser sends the request:
Authorization: Bearer attacker_token
Now the client app unknowingly uses the attacker’s token.
The system becomes confused about whose identity is actually being used.

-> Why this matters?
Data gets stored or actions executed under the wrong identity.
Example:
Victim uploads sensitive documents.
They get stored in attacker’s account.

->Mitigation:-
• Applications must bind tokens to:
	user session
	client identity
	state parameter
• Tokens should never be blindly accepted from the browser.

10. Why is dynamic client registration risky?
OAuth allows dynamic client registration.
a) First understand what a “client” means in OAuth:
In OAuth terminology, a client is simply an application that wants users to log in through the identity provider.
Example:
User
 │
 ▼
Application (OAuth Client)
 │
 ▼
Authorization Server

Examples of clients:
• a web application
• a mobile app
• a SaaS integration
• a third-party plugin

Before a client can use OAuth, it must register with the authorization server.
During registration the client gets:
client_id
client_secret
And it must provide a redirect URI.

Example registration:
Client Name: PhotoApp
Redirect URI: https://photoapp.com/oauth/callback

b) What happens in normal (safe) client registration:
In most companies the registration is manual.
Example:
Developer requests OAuth access
        │
        ▼
Admin approves
        │
        ▼
Client registered
So the identity provider knows who the client is.

c) What dynamic client registration means:
Dynamic registration means clients can register automatically through an API.
Instead of an admin approving it, a client simply calls:
POST /register
Example request:
{
 "client_name": "MyApp",
 "redirect_uris": ["https://myapp.com/callback"]
}
The authorization server automatically creates:
client_id = xyz123
client_secret = abc987
Now the client can immediately use OAuth.
This feature was created to support automated integrations and SaaS ecosystems.

d) Why this becomes dangerous:
If anyone on the internet can register clients, attackers can create malicious OAuth applications.
Example attacker registration:
client_name = Google Login Helper
redirect_uri = https://evil.com/callback
The authorization server will happily create the client.
client_id = attacker123
The Identity Provider now trusts this client.

e) Now the attacker abuses OAuth login:
Instead of an application triggering OAuth, the attacker just builds the URL themselves.
The attacker sends a phishing link:
Example phishing message:

Security update required.
Please login to verify your account:
https://auth.company.com/authorize?client_id=attacker123

The victim clicks the link.
The victim’s browser opens:
auth.company.com
This is the real company identity provider.
So the victim sees the legitimate login page from the legitimate identity provider.
. Company Login Portal
. Username
. Password
The victim thinks:"This is my company's login page.".
So the victim logs in with Company Account.
Everything looks normal.

f) What happens after login:
After authentication the authorization server sends the authorization code to the registered redirect URI.
But that redirect URI belongs to the attacker.
https://evil.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
Now the attacker receives the authorization code.

g) The attacker exchanges the code for tokens
The attacker sends:
POST /token
grant_type=authorization_code
code=AUTH_CODE
client_id=attacker123

Now the attacker receives
access_token
id_token
The victim has unknowingly granted access.

-> Why this fools users easily:
The victim sees:
accounts.company.com
This is the real identity provider.
So they trust it.
They don't realize the OAuth client is malicious.

-> Why large companies restrict this?
Companies heavily restrict client registration.
They require:
• app verification
• domain ownership checks
• security review
• restricted scopes
Without this, OAuth becomes a phishing platform.

-> How systems mitigate this risk:
Secure systems implement controls like:
• Restricted registration
• Only trusted developers can create clients.
• Redirect URI validation
• Domains must be verified.
• User consent screens
• Users see exactly which app is requesting access.
Example:
PhotoApp wants to access your profile
App verification
Identity providers review apps before allowing sensitive scopes.


-> The Big Mental Model for OAuth / OIDC Misuse:
Most OAuth vulnerabilities fall into five core categories:
1. Redirect manipulation:
Token/code leakage through redirects.
2. Token misuse:
Using ID tokens where access tokens should be used.
3. Token validation failures:
Missing validation of:
aud
iss
exp
nonce
signature
4. Browser-based leaks:
Sensitive data exposed via:
URLs
referer headers
logs
5. Protocol confusion:
Mixing identities, tokens, or clients.

• Most OAuth and OIDC vulnerabilities occur when applications treat tokens as simple credentials rather than verifying their intended audience, context, and binding to the original authentication request.

-> Use Cases:

1. Open Redirect + OAuth Authorization Code Theft:
This is one of the classic OAuth kill chains.
Architecture:

User Browser
     │
     ▼
Application
     │
     ▼
Authorization Server

The application registers this redirect URI:
https://app.com/oauth/callback
But the application also has an open redirect endpoint:
https://app.com/redirect?url=

-> Step-by-step attack:
The attacker crafts this OAuth request:
https://auth.company.com/authorize?
 client_id=app123
 &redirect_uri=https://app.com/redirect?url=https://evil.com
 &response_type=code
The victim logs in.
The Identity Provider sends the authorization code to the registered redirect URI:
https://app.com/redirect?url=https://evil.com&code=AUTH_CODE
The application immediately redirects the browser to the attacker.
https://evil.com?code=AUTH_CODE
Now the attacker exchanges the code for tokens.

-> Why this works:
Three things combine:
	1. OAuth redirect trust
	2. Application open redirect
	3. Code sent via URL
Each issue alone seems minor, but together they leak the authorization code.

2. OAuth Token Confusion Across Microservices
This attack appears in large microservice environments.
Architecture

                ┌──────────────┐
                │ Auth Server  │
                └──────┬───────┘
                       │
         ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
         ▼             ▼             ▼
    Profile API   Payment API    Admin API
Each service expects tokens for itself.
Example token:
aud = profile-api

-> Misconfiguration:
Some APIs validate only:
signature
issuer
But ignore the audience claim.

-> Attack chain:
	1. Attacker obtains a token for a low-privilege API.
aud = profile-api
	2. The attacker sends it to another API.
GET /admin/deleteUser
Authorization: Bearer profile_token
	3. The admin API accepts the token because it only checks signature.

Result:
Privilege escalation across services.
This attack is called token audience confusion.

3. Referer Leakage + Third-Party Scripts + Code Exchange
This attack combines browser behavior with OAuth flow design.
Architecture:

User Browser
     │
     ▼
Application callback page
     │
     ├── analytics.js
     ├── CDN scripts
     └── images
The OAuth callback URL looks like this:
https://app.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE

-> Attack chain:
The callback page loads external analytics:
<script src="https://tracker.com/script.js">
The browser requests the script:
GET /script.js
Host: tracker.com
Referer: https://app.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The third-party domain now sees the authorization code.
If the attacker controls or compromises that service, they steal the code and exchange it.

-> Why this attack works:
Three things combine:
	1. OAuth code in URL
	2. Browser referer behavior
	3. Third-party resources

4. ID Token Misuse + Missing Audience Validation
This chain appears frequently in OIDC integrations.
Architecture

User
 │
 ▼
Web Client
 │
 ▼
API Gateway
 │
 ▼
Backend APIs
The client logs in with OpenID Connect and receives:
ID token
Access token

-> Misconfiguration:-
The API gateway accepts ID tokens as authentication.
Developers think:
JWT == valid identity
But they never check the audience claim.

-> Attack chain:
	1. Attacker authenticates using another application.
	2. They receive an ID token.
aud = other-client
	3. They send the ID token to the API.
Authorization: Bearer ID_TOKEN
	4. The gateway accepts it.

Result:-
The attacker authenticates to services they were never meant to access.

5. OAuth Client Impersonation + Wildcard Redirect URI
This is one of the most dangerous OAuth design mistakes.
Architecture

Identity Provider
        │
        ▼
Multiple OAuth Clients
Client registration allows wildcard redirects:
https://*.app.com/callback

-> Misconfiguration:-
The authorization server allows:
redirect_uri=https://anything.app.com/callback

-> Attack chain:-
The attacker registers a domain:
evil.app.com
Then starts OAuth:
https://auth.company.com/authorize?
 client_id=app123
 &redirect_uri=https://evil.app.com/callback
The user logs in.
The authorization server sends the code to the attacker domain.
https://evil.app.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The attacker exchanges it for tokens.

-> Why this happens:
Wildcard redirect validation creates implicit trust in subdomains.
But attackers can sometimes control subdomains through:
• subdomain takeover
• DNS misconfiguration
• cloud bucket hosting

